.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt GETUID 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getuid ,
.Nm geteuid ,
.Nm getgid ,
.Nm getegid ,
.Nm getresuid ,
.Nm getresgid ,
.Nm getgroups ,
.Nm getuid32 ,
.Nm geteuid32 ,
.Nm getgid32 ,
.Nm getegid32 ,
.Nm getresuid32 ,
.Nm getresgid32 ,
.Nm getgroups32
.Nd get process credentials
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _GNU_SOURCE"
.In unistd.h
.Ft uid_t
.Fn getuid void
.Ft uid_t
.Fn geteuid void
.Ft gid_t
.Fn getgid void
.Ft gid_t
.Fn getegid void
.Ft int
.Fn getgroups "int size" "gid_t list[]"
.Ft int
.Fn getresuid "uid_t *ruid" "uid_t *euid" "uid_t *suid"
.Ft int
.Fn getresgid "gid_t *rgid" "gid_t *egid" "gid_t *sgid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions read the calling process's credentials.
The initial user and group IDs are all 0, for root, until a login server
changes them.
.Pp
.Fn getuid
and
.Fn geteuid
return the real and effective user IDs, respectively.
.Fn getgid
and
.Fn getegid
return the real and effective group IDs.
.Pp
.Fn getresuid
writes the real, effective, and saved user IDs through
.Fa ruid ,
.Fa euid ,
and
.Fa suid .
.Fn getresgid
writes the corresponding group IDs through
.Fa rgid ,
.Fa egid ,
and
.Fa sgid .
These two declarations require
.Dv _GNU_SOURCE .
The pointers are written in that order; a failed copy may leave earlier
results written.
.Pp
.Fn getgroups
copies the supplementary group IDs to
.Fa list ,
which has room for
.Fa size
entries.
With
.Fa size
0, it returns the number of entries without accessing
.Fa list .
The kernel stores at most eight supplementary groups.
The effective group ID is not added to this list.
.Ss System call numbers
Musl uses the i386 32-bit ID calls:
199 for
.Nm getuid32 ,
201 for
.Nm geteuid32 ,
200 for
.Nm getgid32 ,
202 for
.Nm getegid32 ,
209 for
.Nm getresuid32 ,
211 for
.Nm getresgid32 ,
and 205 for
.Nm getgroups32 .
It declares the functions without the
.Ql 32
suffix and provides no separate prototypes for these kernel names.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn getuid ,
.Fn geteuid ,
.Fn getgid ,
and
.Fn getegid
return the requested ID and have no error return.
.Fn getresuid
and
.Fn getresgid
return 0 on success.
.Fn getgroups
returns the number of supplementary groups.
On failure, the latter three functions return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/credprobe.c
sets supplementary groups 1000 and 2000, reads them back, queries their
count with size 0, and checks that size 1 gives
.Er EINVAL .
It also changes the effective user ID to 1000 while keeping real user
ID 0, then checks that
.Fn getresuid
reports real ID 0 and effective and saved IDs 1000.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
An output pointer for
.Fn getresuid
or
.Fn getresgid ,
or a required entry of
.Fa list
for
.Fn getgroups ,
cannot be written in user memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn getgroups
was given a negative
.Fa size
or a nonzero size smaller than the supplementary group count.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setuid 2
